\end{aligned}$

The period of a simple pendulum is given by
$\begin{aligned}
& T=2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}} \\
& \therefore T \propto \sqrt{L}
\end{aligned}$
If the clock is running slow, that means time period is larger as compared to the correct value. So, by reducing the length of the pendulum the time period can be shortened to get the correct value.

From Stefan's law, the total radiant energy emitted per second per unit surface area of a black body is proportional to the fourth power of the absolute temperature $(T)$ of the body $E=\sigma T^4$
Given, $T_1=273^{\circ} \mathrm{C}=273+273 \mathrm{~K}=564 \mathrm{~K}T_2=0^0 \mathrm{C}=273 \mathrm{~K}\begin{aligned}
& \therefore \frac{E_1}{E_2}=\frac{T_1^4}{T_2^4} \\
& \Rightarrow E_2=\frac{T_2^4}{T_1^4} E_1=\frac{(273)^4}{(564)^4} R=\frac{R}{16}
\end{aligned}$

